## Intro

### databricks CLI bundles
Resources in this project are configured and deployed through [databricks CLI bundles]({{ template `generate_doc_link` (map (pair "path" "dev-tools/cli/bundle-cli.html")) }}).
The bundle setting file must be expressed in YAML format and must contain at minimum the top-level bundle mapping.

The databricks CLI bundles top level is defined by file `{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}/databricks.yml`.
During databricks CLI bundles deployment, the root config file will be loaded, validated and deployed to workspace provided by the environment together with all the included resources.

## What is in this directory

```
resources/
├── {{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}.yml          <- GENERATED. Job for the Kedro `__default__` pipeline.
├── ml-artifacts-resource.yml   <- Unity Catalog registered model and MLflow experiment.
└── monitoring/
    └── monitoring-resource.yml <- Opt-in quality monitor + retraining job.
```

`databricks.yml` includes `./resources/*.yml`, so any file added at this level is picked
up automatically. That is why the opt-in monitoring resource sits in a subdirectory: it
is enabled by uncommenting its own `include` entry in `databricks.yml`, once the
inference table it monitors exists.

### The pipeline job

`{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}.yml` defines one Databricks job
with one task per Kedro node, wired together by the pipeline's own node dependencies.
Each task loads the wheel that `kedro package` builds into `../dist` and calls the
console script declared in `pyproject.toml` under `[project.scripts]`:

```
--nodes <node name> --conf-source /Workspace/${workspace.file_path}/conf --env ${var.environment}
```

`${var.environment}` resolves to the deployment target's name, so a task running under
target `staging` reads `conf/base` layered with `conf/staging`.

## Regenerating the pipeline jobs

The pipeline job files are **generated**, not hand-maintained. After adding, renaming or
reordering Kedro nodes, regenerate them:

```bash
kedro databricks bundle --overwrite --env dev
```

This reads the pipeline definitions from `src/{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}/pipelines`,
merges the overrides from `conf/<target>/databricks.yml`, and writes one file per
registered pipeline. Expect two files:

| File | Kedro pipeline | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}.yml` | `__default__` | Scheduled daily. Committed with this template. |
| `{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}_iris.yml` | `iris` | Not committed. `__default__` is the sum of all pipelines, so while `iris` is the only pipeline this job runs exactly the same nodes; deploying both would create two equivalent jobs. Delete it, or commit it once you have more than one pipeline. |

Review `git diff resources/` after regenerating. Anything hand-added directly to a
generated file — job permissions, notifications, extra tasks — is **discarded**, because
the generator rebuilds the file from the pipeline plus the `conf/<target>/databricks.yml`
overrides. Nothing else is consulted.

The generator also writes YAML without comments, so the explanatory header at the top of
`{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}.yml` disappears on the first
regeneration. That header is the only expected diff for an unchanged pipeline: if
`git diff` shows anything else, something in the pipeline or the overrides has moved.

## Where to change what

| To change | Edit |
|---|---|
| Which nodes run, and in what order | `src/{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}/pipelines/…`, then regenerate |
| Cluster size, Spark version, Spark env vars | `conf/<target>/databricks.yml`, then regenerate |
| Job name or schedule | `conf/<target>/databricks.yml` (per-workflow keys), then regenerate |
| Job permissions | `permissions:` at the top level of `databricks.yml` — a job-level block would not survive regeneration, and DAB rejects the same job key being split across two resource files |
| Workspace hosts, targets, catalogs | `databricks.yml` |
| Dataset paths per environment | `conf/<target>/catalog.yml` |
| Registered model / experiment | `ml-artifacts-resource.yml` |

## Local development and dev workspace

### Set up authentication

To set up the databricks CLI using a Databricks personal access token, take the following steps:

1. Follow [databricks CLI]({{ template `generate_doc_link` (map (pair "path" "dev-tools/cli/databricks-cli.html")) }}) to download and set up the databricks CLI locally.
2. Complete the `TODO` in `databricks.yml` to add the dev workspace URL under `targets.dev.workspace.host` if it is not already correct for your organisation.
3. Set up authentication:

```
$ databricks configure --host <your host>
```

### Validate and provision resources

1. After installing the databricks CLI and creating the token, you can validate the bundle:

```
$ databricks bundle validate
```

2. Then deploy the resources to your dev workspace. `databricks.yml` declares an
   `artifacts` entry that runs `kedro package` for you, so the wheel is rebuilt on
   every deploy:

```
$ databricks bundle deploy --target dev
```

3. Trigger a run without waiting for the schedule:

```
$ databricks bundle run {{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}} --target dev
```

### Destroy resources

To destroy the resources you have provisioned:

```
$ databricks bundle destroy --target dev
```

## Develop and test config changes

Because the pipeline jobs are generated, the loop for a config change is:

1. Edit `conf/<target>/databricks.yml` (compute, schedule, job name) or the pipeline itself.
2. `kedro databricks bundle --overwrite --env dev`
3. `git diff resources/` and confirm the change is the only one.
4. `databricks bundle validate --target dev`
5. `databricks bundle deploy --target dev`

For changes to `databricks.yml`, `ml-artifacts-resource.yml` or the monitoring resource,
skip step 2 — those files are hand-maintained.

To check what a target resolves to without deploying, use:

```
$ databricks bundle validate --target staging --output json
```
